00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:11.810 align:start position:0%
the dense tropical rainforests of  Liberia are a haven for the closest of  human relatives the chimpanzee but as  the nation recovers from years of civil  war  the chimps habitat is emptying fast  experts say most chimps in the wild will  be extinct in the next few decades due  to a thriving trade in bush meat I  recently visited Liberia to join  primatologist hail Markel from the Max  Planck Institute for evolutionary  anthropology he and his colleagues set  up the pan Africa great a program in  Liberia which is the first of 15 nations  to host a systematic survey of their  chimpanzee populations with such a grave  full cast for the chimpanzee this  project aims to gain as much information  about them as possible  before they disappear the Liberian  landscape can be treacherous local teams  often have to struggle through uncut  bush across raging rivers and over roads  which become mud traps in the rainy  season in South Pole National Park  Liberia's only protected reserve  volunteer Vera Lynette has set up video  cameras to observe the chimps in their  natural environment these cameras are  triggered by infrared motion sensors as  chimps pass by though Vera's been in  South Pole for a year now she's yet to  see a single chimp face-to-face on the  plus side capturing footage of these  playful creatures has enabled her to  really get to know the chimp community  this kind of data is proving invaluable  for studying chimps health in the wild  and a shedding light on concepts such as  animal culture reporting for nature  video and guides rewriting arson  you
 

